questions.question:
Given the generic class:

public class Box<T>
{
    public event EventHandler<T> Changed;
}

If you create Box<string> box = new Box<string>(); which of the following method signatures can be used to subscribe to box.Changed?

questions.expected_answer:
public interface ILogger { void Log(string message); }
public class ConsoleLogger : ILogger { public void Log(string message) => Console.WriteLine(message); }
public class NullLogger : ILogger { public void Log(string message) { } }
public static class LoggerFactory { public static ILogger Create(string type) => type == "Console" ? new ConsoleLogger() : new NullLogger(); }
// Usage:
var logger = LoggerFactory.Create("Console");
logger.Log("Hello");

questions.user_answer:
using System;

public interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message);
}

public class ConsoleLogger : ILogger
{
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message);
    }
}

public class NullLogger : ILogger
{
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        // intentionally do nothing
    }
}

public static class LoggerFactory
{
    public static ILogger Create(string type)
    {
        if (type == "Console")
            return new ConsoleLogger();

        return new NullLogger();
    }
}

questions.question:
Debug the memory issue: why are Subscriber instances not collected, and fix the code so they can be garbage-collected?

Code:

class Publisher {
    public event EventHandler SomethingHappened;
    public void Raise() => SomethingHappened?.Invoke(this, EventArgs.Empty);
}

class Subscriber {
    public Subscriber(Publisher p) { p.SomethingHappened += Handle; }
    void Handle(object s, EventArgs e) { /* do work */ }
}

static void Main() {
    var pub = new Publisher();
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        new Subscriber(pub);
    }
    GC.Collect();
    GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();
    Console.WriteLine("Done");
}

What is the bug and show a minimal code change to fix it so Subscriber objects can be collected?

questions.expected_answer:
Bug: The Publisher's event SomethingHappened holds strong references to Subscriber.Handle, preventing the Subscriber instances from being garbage-collected. Fix: unsubscribe the handler when the subscriber is no longer needed (or implement IDisposable and unsubscribe in Dispose). Minimal fix (store publisher reference and unsubscribe):

class Subscriber {
    private Publisher _pub;
    public Subscriber(Publisher p) { _pub = p; _pub.SomethingHappened += Handle; }
    public void Unsubscribe() { _pub.SomethingHappened -= Handle; _pub = null; }
    void Handle(object s, EventArgs e) { /* do work */ }
}

// Usage in Main:
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    var s = new Subscriber(pub);
    s.Unsubscribe(); // allows GC to collect
}

Alternatively implement IDisposable on Subscriber and call Dispose (or use a weak event pattern).

questions.user_answer:
class Subscriber
{
    Publisher _p;

    public Subscriber(Publisher p)
    {
        _p = p;
        _p.SomethingHappened += Handle;
    }

    ~Subscriber()
    {
        _p.SomethingHappened -= Handle;  // allow collection
    }

    void Handle(object s, EventArgs e) { /* do work */ }
}

questions.question:
Debug the following C# code (generics + events). It fails to compile. Find and fix the bug so subscribers receive the processed item.

using System;

public class ItemEventArgs<T> : EventArgs
{
    public T Item { get; }
    public ItemEventArgs(T item) { Item = item; }
}

public class Processor<T>
{
    public event EventHandler<ItemEventArgs<T>> ItemProcessed;

    public void Process(T item)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Processing " + item.ToString());
        ItemProcessed?.Invoke(this, item); // BUG
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var p = new Processor<string>();
        p.ItemProcessed += (s, e) => Console.WriteLine("Received: " + e.Item);
        p.Process("Hello");
    }
}

What is the bug and provide the corrected line(s).

questions.expected_answer:
Bug: EventHandler<ItemEventArgs<T>> expects an ItemEventArgs<T> as the second argument, but code passes item (type T). Fix by creating and passing a new ItemEventArgs<T>. Corrected line in Process:

ItemProcessed?.Invoke(this, new ItemEventArgs<T>(item));

(Optionally acceptable alternative: change the event to Action<T> and invoke ItemProcessed?.Invoke(item), but the primary fix is to pass ItemEventArgs<T>.)

questions.user_answer:
Bug 1 — ItemEventArgs is missing a type parameter
Bug 2 — Processor must also be generic
Bug 3 — Event type is wrong
Bug 4 — Wrong event invocation argument
corrected code: using System;

public class ItemEventArgs<T> : EventArgs
{
    public T Item { get; }
    public ItemEventArgs(T item) { Item = item; }
}

public class Processor<T>
{
    public event EventHandler<ItemEventArgs<T>> ItemProcessed;

    public void Process(T item)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Processing " + item);
        ItemProcessed?.Invoke(this, new ItemEventArgs<T>(item));
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var p = new Processor<string>();
        p.ItemProcessed += (s, e) => Console.WriteLine("Received: " + e.Item);
